After much thinking, I've decided... we need a new Nethack/Slash'EM 
interface. (Warning: this is a REALLY long post, so if you don't feel 
like reading it... yeah.)

I mean, the main reason I can't get some of my friends to try Nethack is 
because the graphics are so bad.

I mean, what do we have right now? Basically, we have the 
standard/native tiled version, GTK+ Hack, Falcon/Vulture's Eye/Claw, and 
noeGNUd.

Let's look at each problem individually.

(I'll make a website with illustrations to what I mean later... much 
later, because I can't find a good host right now)

  1. Tiles

The tiles themselves. The standard tiled interface's tiles are actually 
pretty decent, although so extremely small they don't fit in with other 
games of our times. The GTK+ Hack tiles aren't very good, Vulture's 
Eye's graphics are probably the best, and noeGNUd looks rather... ugly, 
especially for 3D.

The best way to do this would probably be to use tiles similar to the 
ones in Vulture's Eye/Claw, except without the carpet, which just looks 
weird, especially in the Mines.

  2. 3D

Right now, the native/standard tiled interface is 2D. GTK+Hack is 3D 
isometric, fixed at something like a 22 degree angle. Vulture's Eye is 
isometric as well, at a not-as-pretty 45 degree angle (which also makes 
movement a bit awkward). And noeGNUd is true 3D, I think with OpenGL or 
something.

The best way to do this would be to have an angle like GTK+Hack, which 
would make it look pretty, yet not make movement awkward.

  3. Facing

I know, I know, it's impossible/insanely hard to implement facing into 
NetHack. But it's not impossible to implement it in the interface. Make 
monsters and the player be generated facing forward (towards the 
screen), then face the direction they last moved in. Monsters fleeing 
would always face away from the player, or we could get YAFMs where 
rogues get the message "You strike [slow monster] from behind" even 
though [slow monster] is facing them. This would create a use for the 
arrow keys in controlling movement - left and right would change the 
direction you're facing (without using any time, of course), and up and 
down would move in/away from the direction you're facing. This could be 
changed back to its default behavior, of course. An arrow would point in 
the direction the playr is facing, so someone polymorphed into, say, a 
lichen, wouldn't have to wonder where it was facing (it could happen, 
like from a polymorph trap).

  4. Controls: Extended commands

The controls to most interfaces are the same, except Falcon's Eye and 
noeGNUd are easier to control with the mouse. Nethack has a pretty good 
extended command interface, Falcon's Eye and GTK+ Hack have terrible 
extended command interfaces (requiring clicking on extended commands 
that have the same first character as other extended commands). And 
noeGNUd has an okay extended command interface, which lets you type out 
the extended command until it narrows it down to one command. However, 
it immediately does the command without needing to press Enter, which, 
if you don't know how many characters to type, can result in 
accidentally doing something you didn't want to do because you forgot to 
stop typing after, say, getting to an arbitrary point in the word "monster".

The best way to do extended commands this would be to have a dialog box 
with the many buttons like in GTK+ Hack/Falcon's Eye/noeGNUd, except 
with a textbox that is autofocused at the top. As you type in the 
textbox, the buttons with commands that don't start with the letters 
currently in the textbox would become greyed-out (but still clickable). 
If Enter is pressed at any time, it would act as if the first button not 
greyed-out were clicked. If only one button is left un-greyed out, the 
remainder of the command will be pasted into the text-box and selected 
(If you've used some forms of auto-complete, you'll know what I mean).

  5. Controls: Miscellaneous

Pressing a key should skip any animaion queued up. In large rooms like 
the Bigroom, just taking a step can take long periods of time, 
especially if you have the "resist stuff" animation on, which takes even 
longer. This goes for nearly every graphical interface.

  6. Animation: Projectiles, Missiles, Spells, and the like

As we all know, projectiles, like arrows, seem to move 'sideways' in 
every know interface, even noeGNUd, which is perfectly capable of fixing 
this problem. I suggest having 8 different images for each weapon, each 
facing a different direction, to use when it is thrown. Perhaps 16 for 
boomerangs and hammers, so we can watch them spin. I doubt this will be 
too much to ask of our modern-day computers with 160GB of hard disk 
space. Okay, maybe not everyone has 160GB of hard disk space, but... 
Let's see how mu... O_O 200 MB for a 16-bit 64x64 bitmap? That's crazy! 
Something's clearly wrong with this calculator. Let's see... MS Paint 
tells me a 24-bit 64x64 bitmap is 12KB, so multiply by 8 and we get 
100KB, multiply by approximately 500 items, and we get... 50MB. 
That's... a tenth of a CD... that's a lot. Of course, we won't be using 
bitmaps, we'll be using PNGs, which would instead take up a total of 
around 1.5 MB, which is about the size of a floppy disk. That's nothing, 
considering the graphics for most modern-day games are at around 500 MB. 
(That's also 1/50 the size of noeGNUd's complete image files).

Spells and wand rays could be more realistic, as well, Cone of Cold 
should look like... a cone. Of cold. Not a blue line. And fireball 
should look like... a fiery ball. Not a red line. And Magic Missile 
should look like... a... magical missile. Not a purple line. Above all, 
a digging beam should stay on the screen for more than a few 
milliseconds, so I can actually notice it without someone telling me 
that a digging beam is actually visible.

  7. Animation: Movement/Combat

Currently, in all interfaces, you basically teleport from square to 
square. Now, I've done enough programming to know it's not that hard to 
animate actually in a pixel-by-pixel basis instead of a tile-by-tile 
basis, especially on todays machines. I mean, if a ten-year-old game can 
have a reasonably fast full 3D engine, we should be able to do this by 
now. In fact, we should have three or four tiles for every monster, one 
for when it's not moving, two for when it's moving, and one for when 
it's attacking (this should take all of 300 KB). Same goes for combat, I 
want to see my sword fly around when I attack someone.

   8. Purple worms.

Purple worms that take up multiple squares don't look very good in the 
normal tiled interface or the GTK+ interface. Purple worms look like... 
well, they look like Knights in Vulture's Eye. This reminds me, they 
have a special image for succubi, yet purple worms look like Knights! 
Who makes these images, sex-deprived children?

...

I'm sorry I asked.

Anyway, you know what purple worms should look like. Make them look like 
that.

   9. Miscellaneous Graphics/Animation

I want to watch my character go down the stairs, and watch my pets and 
intelligent monsters follow. When I get the message "Archie casts a 
spell. Archie turns transparent" because he's cast "Disappear" (That'd 
be the monster version of Make Invisible), I want to see Archie turn 
transparent! When I anger my god really badly while in Gehennom, I want 
the lightning strike and massive wide-angle disintegration beam to 
look... like the kind of stuff you'd expect from a god! When I pray and 
get the message "you are basked in a blue light", I want to see some 
blue light!

...you get the picture.

I also want to see things like birds flying around my head when I'm 
stunned, a question mark looming above my head when I'm confused, Z's to 
fly around my head when I'm sleeping, and stuff like that.

   End of list.

Well, there's probably more, but that's all I can think of right now. 
Discuss what I have while I think of all the stuff I left out.

